{"key":"rain","updated":"2026-11-25","label":"Rainfall","title":"Rainfall compared with normal","subtitle":"Aug–Oct 2026, against the same months in 1991–2020","source":"gridMET daily precipitation, averaged by parish","demo":false,"build":1,"categories":[{"key":"very_dry","label":"Very dry","color":"#8c510a","note":"driest 10% of years"},{"key":"dry","label":"Dry","color":"#d8b365","note":"driest 10 to 30% of years"},{"key":"normal","label":"Near normal","color":"#f3efe4","note":"middle 40% of years"},{"key":"wet","label":"Wet","color":"#5ab4ac","note":"wettest 10 to 30% of years"},{"key":"very_wet","label":"Very wet","color":"#01665e","note":"wettest 10% of years"},{"key":"not_rated","label":"Not rated","note":"too few years or days missing","color":"#bdbdbd"}],"validation":"rain","rank":{"high":"Wettest compared with normal","low":"Driest compared with normal","note":"Ranked by rain as a percentage of normal."},"how":"Rain over the last three complete months (Aug–Oct 2026) compared with the same three months in each year from 1991 to 2020. A parish is \"very dry\" when this year is among the driest 10% of those years and \"dry\" when it is among the driest 30%. Wet works the same way in the other direction.","limits":["The values come from gridMET, a 4 km grid that mixes PRISM (built from rain gauges) with a land model. It is an estimate of rain, not a gauge reading.","A parish value is the average of the grid cells inside it, so a storm over part of a big parish gets smoothed out.","The newest days are provisional and get revised for a few weeks. A month only counts when every day is in the record.","Normal here means 1991 to 2020. A value of 70% means 30% less rain than the average for those months. The map says nothing about whether soil or crops are short of water.","The table under the map compares gridMET with the station gauges where both have data."],"credits":[{"text":"Rainfall: gridMET, Abatzoglou, J.T. (2013), Int. J. Climatology 33: 121-131","url":"ht